---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../lib/contact';
import { UNREVIEWED_PAGES } from '../lib/seo/unreviewed-pages';

const path = '/privacy';
---
<BaseLayout title="Privacy" description="RayValue privacy policy: no cookies, privacy-friendly analytics, and what happens when you request installer quotes." path={path} noindex={UNREVIEWED_PAGES.has(path)}>
  <div class="page">
    <!-- TODO: owner legal review -->
    <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Privacy' }]} />
    <h1 class="!mt-4">Privacy</h1>
    <p>This is a plain description of what RayValue does with data today.</p>

    <h2>No cookies</h2>
    <p>RayValue does not set cookies and does not use advertising or cross-site tracking.</p>

    <h2>Analytics</h2>
    <p>
      We use Vercel Web Analytics to count page views. It does not use cookies and reports only aggregated
      numbers, such as how many people viewed a page. We send page addresses without their query string, so the
      ZIP code and bill amount in a shared calculator link are not part of it.
    </p>

    <h2>The calculator</h2>
    <p>
      Your ZIP code, bill and other answers are used in your browser to run the estimate; we do not save them in a
      database unless you request installer quotes (see below). To look up how much sun your area gets, the rounded center point of your ZIP code (not your address)
      is sent to our server, which asks the PVWatts service run by the National Laboratory of the Rockies (formerly
      NREL). If you copy a link to your estimate, your answers are part of that link.
    </p>

    <h2>Service providers</h2>
    <p>
      These companies store or process data on our behalf, only to run RayValue: Vercel hosts the site and provides
      the cookieless analytics described above (as with any website, it processes request data such as IP addresses
      to serve pages and protect the site from abuse), and Supabase hosts our database, where quote requests, privacy
      requests, estimate-email requests and alert signups are stored. If you choose to confirm your phone number with a
      text-message code, Twilio sends and checks that code. If you ask us to email you an estimate, an email delivery
      service sends it. <!-- OWNER: name the email provider here once chosen (RESEND_API_KEY / EMAIL_FROM). -->
    </p>

    <h2>Quote requests</h2>
    <p>
      If you ask for installer quotes, we store what you enter in the quote form in our database (hosted by
      Supabase): your first and last name, email address, mobile phone number, street address, ZIP code and state,
      whether you own the home, your approximate monthly electric bill and utility, your roof&rsquo;s shade and age, whether you
      want a battery, how you would like to pay, and when you hope to go solar. When you send the form from your
      calculator results, we also store your calculator answers (utility, monthly bill, battery and shade) and the
      estimate you saw.
    </p>
    <p>
      We also store a record of your consent: the exact consent wording you saw (including the names of the
      installers it listed), its version, when you agreed, your IP address, the page you sent the request from and
      your browser&rsquo;s user-agent string. If we use a consent-verification service for this, it may also record
      your visit to the form and give us a certificate of it; we will update this page before turning one on.
    </p>
    <p>
      We use this to connect you with solar installers. We may sell or share your quote request with the solar
      installers named in the consent wording you agreed to (up to 3 serving your ZIP code), and with no one else
      for marketing. Those installers and RayValue may contact you about solar quotes, including by calls and texts
      made with automated technology. Consent is not a condition of purchase. Apart from that, it is processed only by
      our service providers listed above. We keep it until you ask us to delete it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>If you rent</strong>, we do not send your request to installers, and the answers you entered in the
      quote form are not sent to us or saved. <strong>If no installer serves your ZIP code yet</strong>, we still save
      your request, with your consent wording, so we can email you when one does; it is not shared with installers
      until then.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Phone confirmation.</strong> If we offer a text-message code, we send it to the number you gave and record
      only whether you confirmed it. Message and data rates may apply. You can skip this step.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Where you came from.</strong> When you arrive on the site, your browser remembers (in session storage,
      not a cookie, and only until you close the tab) the campaign details in the link you used, such as utm_source or
      a Google or Facebook click ID, the first page you landed on and the website that sent you, without any query
      string. If you then request quotes, we save these with your request to learn which of our pages and campaigns
      work, and installers may see them.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>What we do with a request.</strong> We record which installers received your request, when, and how the
      delivery went. If you send us more than one request with the same phone number or email within 30 days, we keep
      the repeat but do not send it on again. If you have asked us not to sell or share your information, or to delete
      it, we do not send any request linked to that email address or phone number to installers. We email the site
      owner a short summary of each request (no name, email address or phone number).
    </p>

    <h2>Email me this estimate</h2>
    <p>
      If you ask us to email your estimate, we store your email address, your state, the estimate you saw, the consent
      wording you agreed to, when you agreed and your IP address. We use it to send the estimate and, because you
      agreed, a short series of up to five emails over about three weeks about solar costs and savings. Every email has
      a one-click unsubscribe link; when you use it we keep your email address on a suppression list so we do not email
      you again. You can also unsubscribe or ask us to delete your data with the
      <a href="/privacy/request">privacy request form</a>. We do not share your email address with installers for these
      emails.
    </p>

    <h2>California residents</h2>
    <p>
      Because we may sell or share quote requests with installers, you can opt out at any time:
      <a href="/privacy/request?type=do_not_sell">Do not sell or share my personal information</a>. You can also ask
      us what we hold about you, or to correct or delete it, with the same form.
    </p>

    <h2>Rate change alerts</h2>
    <p>
      If you sign up for rate change alerts, we store your email address and your state in our database (hosted by
      Supabase), only to send you those alerts. We do not sell or share it.
    </p>

    <h2>Deleting or seeing your data</h2>
    <p>
      To ask us to delete your data, send you a copy, correct it, or stop sharing it, use our
      <a href="/privacy/request">privacy request form</a>. To stop calls, texts or emails from an installer, you can
      also tell that installer directly.
    </p>
    {CONTACT_EMAIL && (
      <p>You can also email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> from the address you gave us.</p>
    )}
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
